  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nUpper ridging remains situated over the Four Corners region with\ntroughing from Ontario down into the Great Lakes region. At the\nsurface, weak stationary front stretches from northern MO into\nnorth central KS. \n\nSome scattered showers and a few storms have been tracking\nacross northeast KS into parts of the Flint Hills overnight in\nan area of 700mb warm advection. This activity should continue\nto slowly slide southeast early this morning. \n\nAnother very hot day will be in place today across the entire\narea, with most locations seeing highs in the 100-106 range.\nLooking at records, the only site that will have a chance will\nbe Russell, which has a record of 106. We should see dewpoints \nmix-out more today than they did on Sat which should keep heat \nindices from getting at or above 110 degrees. With confidence \nhigh in widespread heat indices around 105 for several hours \ntoday, went ahead and issued a heat advisory for the entire \narea. Front will become more defined late this afternoon and \nearly evening from southeast NE into northwest KS and may \nprovide the focus for a storm or two along or north of I-70 \nafter 22z. Just like late Sat afternoon/early evening, coverage \nwill be minimal with only a storm or two expected if they do \ndevelop. \n\nConfidence is high that hot temps will remain in place for Mon\nwith most locations once again in the 100 to 105 range. While\nthere is a good chance at least part of the area will eventually\nbe put into a heat advisory for Mon, increasing high clouds and\nlow confidence on how much dps will mix out is keeping us from \ngoing with one at this time. Not much is expected to change for \nTue, with all areas once again seeing high at or above the \ncentury mark. For both Mon and Tue, surface front will continue \nto linger between I-70 and the KS/NE border and may provide the \nfocus for a storm or two. With coverage extremely minimal if \nstorms do develop, will leave mention of storms out for now. On \nWed, there remains good agreement in an upper impulse digging \nsoutheast out of southern B.C. with some additional upper energy\nmoving into central CA. This will allow some monsoonal \nperturbations to track over the Central Rockies and out into the\nCentral Plains for Wed night. This will ramp-up shower and \nstorm chances, but it it looks like the higher chances will \nremain north of the forecast area. So we are looking for \nanother very hot day on Wed with highs similar to Mon and Tue. \n\nBroad western CONUS upper troughing will keep fairly high storm\nchances in for Thu-Sat, especially across Nebraska and northern\nportions of KS. Confidence is low on how far south precip and \ncooler temps will make it, as models have trended to keep the \nfront and precip further north, which would result in continued \nhot temps for Thu and possibly Fri, especially for the southern\nportion of the forecast area.",
